<<
/Title (Protocols are rules that devices follow to communicate. They make sure data is sent and received in a standard way. The Internet depends on shared protocols so devices from different companies and systems can work together. Without protocols, devices would not know how to format, send, receive, or interpret data correctly. Shared rules make global communication possible.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 854 0 R
/Next 876 0 R
>>
endobj
873 0 obj
<<
/Title (The Internet uses open standards, which are publicly available rules. These standards let different devices and systems communicate. Because of this design, the Internet is scalable, meaning it can keep growing to support more users, devices, and services. No single company owns the whole Internet. Many organizations manage their own networks, and those networks connect through shared rules and standards.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 854 0 R
/Prev 877 0 R
>>
endobj
